<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahara</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saharan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world, covering most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from the Atlantic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. Trans-Saharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> carried gold, salt, slaves, textiles and ideas between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, sustaining empires on both sides of the desert. It is central to the profiles of the Garamantian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> and Almoravid empires, among many others.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahara.jpg" alt="Sahara" width="320" height="173" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Project Apollo Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sahara_real_color.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…held little beyond Marrakesh and its surroundings, and in 1269 the last caliph, Idris al-Wathiq (Abu Dabbus), was killed in battle against the Marinids, who seized the capital and extinguished the dynasty that had once ruled from the Atlantic to Tunisia and from the <b>Sahara</b> to the Tagus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid military derived its decisive early advantage from the Lamtuna and allied Sanhaja Berbers of the western <b>Sahara</b>, veiled warriors hardened by the desert who brought to battle an endurance, discipline and cohesion that the armies of Morocco and al-Andalus could not match.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Oil was discovered at Hassi Messaoud in the <b>Sahara</b> in 1956, just as the colony entered its final crisis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The federation covered about 4.7 million square kilometres, from the Atlantic to Lake Chad and from the <b>Sahara</b> to the Gulf of Guinea, with a population of well over ten million that approached twenty million by the late 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Settled in the habitable depressions of the Fezzan region of modern Libya, above all the Wadi al-Ajal and the Wadi ash-Shati, the Berber-speaking pastoralists who became the Garamantes faced a fundamental constraint: the <b>Sahara</b>'s surface water had largely vanished with the end of the Green Sahara period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The spread of the camel across the western <b>Sahara</b> in the early centuries CE transformed irregular desert crossings into a structured, sustainable trade network, and Wagadou was already positioned to dominate it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
